Student Academic Performance Analysis

Exploratory data analysis and machine learning project on the UCI Student Performance dataset, applying regression, classification, and clustering to support early academic intervention.

Overview

This project analyzes 395 students' academic and behavioral records to:

Predict final grades (G3) using regression models
Classify students as at-risk of delayed graduation for early intervention
Segment students into behavioral profiles using unsupervised clustering
Dataset
Source: UCI Machine Learning Repository — Student Performance
File used: student-mat.csv (Mathematics course)
Size: 395 students, 33 features (demographics, family background, study habits, grades)
Methods
Task	Techniques
Regression	Linear Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest (tuned via GridSearchCV)
Classification	Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest (class-weighted, tuned via RandomizedSearchCV)
Clustering	PCA, K-Means, Gaussian Mixture Model
Interpretation	SHAP values, feature importance, Mann-Whitney U / chi-square tests
Key Findings
Prior failures and absenteeism were the strongest, most consistent predictors of academic risk across every method tested — confirmed independently through bivariate analysis, feature selection, statistical testing, and SHAP interpretation.
The tuned Random Forest regressor achieved R² = 0.243 predicting final grades from behavioral/demographic data alone (deliberately excluding prior grades G1/G2 to keep the model a genuine early-warning tool).
The tuned classifier achieved 0.53 recall on identifying at-risk students, prioritizing catching struggling students over raw accuracy.
K-Means clustering (k=3) revealed three distinct student segments: High-Achieving/Disciplined, Socially Active/Low Effort, and At-Risk/Attendance-Logistics — each mapped to a different advising intervention.
